    <md:title>1.14.7: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>42681766-9871-4556-8610-64e38fe90418</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para><emphasis><emphasis>Students will complete the following questions to practice the skills they have learned in this lesson.</emphasis></emphasis></para>
<blockquote>
  <para>In questions 1 – 5, find an equation of a line that is parallel to the given line and contains the given point. Write the equation in slope-intercept form.</para>
</blockquote>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>Line \(y = 4x + 2\)
    Point
    \((1, 2)\) </item>
</list>
<list>
  <item> \(y=4x+2\) </item>
  <item> \(y=4x-2\) </item>
  <item> \(y=-¼ x-2\) </item>
  <item> \(y=- ¼ x +2\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(y = 4x -2\)</para>
<para>The slope of a line parallel to \(y = 4x + 2\) is 4. The line contains the point (1,2).</para>
 <!--BEGIN ALIGN TO EQUALS-->
 <para>\(\begin{aligned}y − y_1 &amp;= m(x − x_1)\\
  y − (2) &amp;= 4(x − (1))\\
  y − 2 &amp;= 4(x − 1)\\
  y − 2 &amp;= 4x − 4\\
  y &amp;= 4x − 2
   \end{aligned}\)</para>
 <!--END ALIGN TO EQUALS -->
 
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>Line \(y = -3x -1\)
    Point \((2, -3)\) </item>
</list>
<list>
  <item> \(y=-3x +3\) </item>
  <item> \(y=-3x-3\) </item>
  <item> \(y=&amp;frac13; x +3\) </item>
  <item> \(y=&amp;frac13; x -3\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(y = -3x +3\) </para>
<para>The slope of a line parallel to \(y = −3x − 1\) is \(−3\). The line contains the point (2,−3).</para>

      <!--BEGIN ALIGN TO EQUALS-->
      <para>\(\begin{aligned}y − y_1 &amp;= m(x − x_1)\\
       y − (-3) &amp;= −3(x − (2))\\
       y + 3 &amp;= −3(x − 2)\\
       y +3 &amp;= −3x + 6\\
       y &amp;= −3x +3
        \end{aligned}\)</para>
      <!--END ALIGN TO EQUALS -->

<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>Line \(2x - y = 6\)
    Point \((3, 0)\) </item>
</list>
<list>
  <item> \(y=2x\) </item>
  <item> \(y=-2x+6\) </item>
  <item> \(y=-2x\) </item>
  <item> \(y=2x-6\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(y = 2x - 6\)</para>
<para>The slope of a line parallel to \(2x −y = 6\) is 2. The line contains the point (3,0).</para>

<!--BEGIN ALIGN TO EQUALS-->
<para>\(\begin{aligned}y − y_1 &amp;= m(x − x_1)\\
 y − (0) &amp;= 2(x − (3))\\
y &amp;= 2(x − 3)\\
 y &amp;= 2x − 6\\
  \end{aligned}\)</para>
<!--END ALIGN TO EQUALS -->
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>Line \(x = -4\)
    Point \((-3, -5)\) </item>
</list>
<list>
  <item> \(x=-3\) </item>
  <item> \(y=-5\) </item>
  <item> \(x=-5\) </item>
  <item> \(y=-4\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(x = -3\) </para>
<para> The slope of a line parallel to \(x = −4\) is undefined. The line contains the point (−3,−5).
  The only equation with an undefined slope passing through (−3,−5) is \(x = −3\).</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item>Line \(y = 5\)
    Point \((2, -2)\) </item>
</list>
<list>
  <item> \(x=2\) </item>
  <item> \(x=5\) </item>
  <item> \(y=-2\) </item>
  <item> \(y=2\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(y = -2\) </para>
<para>The slope of a line parallel to \(y = 5\) is 0. The line contains the point (2,−2).
The only equation with a slope of 0 passing through (2, −2) is \(y = −2\).</para>

<blockquote>
  <para>In questions 6 – 10, find an equation of a line that is perpendicular to the given line and contains the given point.  The choices are in
    slope-intercept form. </para>
</blockquote>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="6" type="enumerated">
  <item>Line \(y = -2x +3\)
    Point \((2, 2)\) </item>
</list>
<list>
  <item> \(y=-2x+6\) </item>
  <item> \(y=-½ x+3\) </item>
  <item> \(y=½ x +3\) </item>
  <item><emphasis> \(y=½ x+1\) </emphasis>
  </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(y = \frac{1}{2}x + 1\)</para>
<para>The slope of a line perpendicular to \(y = −2x + 3\) is \(\frac12\). The line contains the point (2,2).</para>

<!--BEGIN ALIGN TO EQUALS-->
<para>\(\begin{aligned}y − y_1 &amp;= m(x − x_1)\\
 y − (2) &amp;= \frac12(x − (2))\\
y −2 &amp;= \frac12(x − 2)\\
 y −2 &amp;= \frac12x − 1\\
 y &amp;= \frac12x + 1
  \end{aligned}\)</para>
<!--END ALIGN TO EQUALS -->
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="7" type="enumerated">
  <item>Line \(y = -x +5\)
    Point \((3, 3)\) </item>
</list>
<list>
  <item> \(y=-x+6\) </item>
  <item> \(y=x\) </item>
  <item> \(y=x+5\) </item>
  <item> \(y=-x+3\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis> \(y = x\)</para>
<para>The slope of a line perpendicular to \(y = −x + 5\) is 1. The line contains the point (3,3).</para>

      <!--BEGIN ALIGN TO EQUALS-->
      <para>\(\begin{aligned}y − y_1 &amp;= m(x − x_1)\\
       y − (3) &amp;= 1(x − (3))\\
       y − 3 &amp;= 1(x − 3)\\
       y − 3 &amp;= x − 3\\
       y &amp;= x \\
        \end{aligned}\)</para>
      <!--END ALIGN TO EQUALS -->
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="8" type="enumerated">
  <item>Line \(y = \frac{3}{4}x - 2\)
    Point \((-3, 4)\) </item>
</list>
<list>
  <item> \(y-\frac43x\) 
  </item>
  <item>\(y-\frac43x+4\)</item>
  <item> \(y=\frac34x+4\) 
  </item>
  <item>\(y=\frac34x+\frac{17}4\)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis> \(y = -\frac{4}{3}x\)</para>
<para> The slope of a line perpendicular to \(y = \frac34x − 2\) is \(−\frac43.\) The line contains the point (−3,4).</para>

<!--BEGIN ALIGN TO EQUALS-->
<para>\(\begin{aligned}y − y_1 &amp;= m(x − x_1)\\
 y − (4) &amp;= −\frac43(x − (−3))\\
 y −4 &amp;= −\frac43(x + 3)\\
 y −4 &amp;= −\frac43x − 4\\
 y &amp;= −\frac43x
  \end{aligned}\)</para>
<!--END ALIGN TO EQUALS -->
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="9" type="enumerated">
  <item>Line \(x = -5\)
    Point \((1, -2)\) </item>
</list>
<list>
  <item> \(y=-5\) </item>
  <item> \(x=1\) </item>
  <item> \(y=-2\) </item>
  <item> \(x=-2\) 
  </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(y= -2\)</para>
<para>The slope of a line perpendicular to \(x = −5\) is 0. The line contains the point (1,−2).
The only equation with a slope of 0 passing through (1,−2) is y \(= −2\).</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="10" type="enumerated">
  <item>Line \(y - 3 = 0\)
    Point \((-2, -4)\) </item>
</list>
<list>
  <item> \(y=33\) </item>
  <item> \(y=-4\) </item>
  <item> \(x=3\) </item>
  <item> \(x=-2\)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis> \(x = -2\) </para>
<para>The slope of a line parallel to \(y −3 = 0\) is undefined. The line contains the point (−2,−4).
The only equation with an undefined slope passing through (−2,−4) is \(x = −2\).</para>
</section>
